"**Answer:** The runner\u2011up received **19,524 votes** in the election that had the largest vote\u2011share gap between the winner and the third\u2011place candidate.\n\n**How we got there**\n\n| Election | Winner (percent\u202f/\u202fvotes) | 2nd place (percent\u202f/\u202fvotes) | 3rd place (percent\u202f/\u202fvotes) | 1st\u202f\u2013\u202f3rd\u202f% gap |\n|----------|--------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------|\n|\u202f2000 Oakville | 47.75\u202f% / 23,073 votes | 27.99\u202f% / 13,524 votes | 19.85\u202f% / 9,594 votes | 27.90\u202f% |\n|\u202f2004 Oakville | 52.01\u202f% / 28,729 votes | 35.35\u202f% / 19,524 votes |  7.29\u202f% / 4,027 votes | **44.72\u202f%** |\n|\u202f2006 Oakville | 43.40\u202f% / 25,892 votes | 42.10\u202f% / 25,148 votes |  9.70\u202f% / 5,815 votes | 33.70\u202f% |\n|\u202f2008 Oakville | 47.00\u202f% / 26,011 votes | 37.10\u202f% / 20,528 votes |  8.50\u202f% / 4,681 votes | 38.50\u202f% |\n|\u202f2011 Oakville | 51.65\u202f% / 30,068 votes | 30.73\u202f% / 17,890 votes | 13.94\u202f% / 8,117 votes | 37.71\u202f% |\n|\u202f2015 Oakville\u202fNorth\u2011Burlington | 46.74\u202f% / 28,415 votes | 43.33\u202f% / 26,342 votes |  7.25\u202f% / 4,405 votes | 39.49\u202f% |\n\nThe **2004 election** produced the largest difference between first and third place (44.72\u202f%), so we look at the runner\u2011up from that election: **Conservative candidate Rick Byers**.\n\n- **Runner\u2011up vote total (2004 Oakville)**: **19,524 votes**\n\n---\n\n### Sources\n\n1. **Oakville 2004 results** \u2013 Wikipedia \u201cOakville (federal electoral district)\u201d:  \n   * Bonnie Brown (Liberal) 52.01\u202f% \u2013 28,729 votes  \n   * Rick Byers (Conservative) 35.35\u202f% \u2013 **19,524 votes** (runner\u2011up)  \n   * Alison Myrden (NDP) 7.29\u202f% \u2013 4,027 votes (third)\u3010commentary: 8\u2020source=8\u2020chunks=7,8\u3011  \n\n2. **Oakville 2000\u20132015 results** \u2013 Stephen Taylor data (for reference on other years):  \n   * 2000: 27.99\u202f% \u2013 13,524 votes (runner\u2011up)  \n   * 2004: 35.35\u202f% \u2013 19,524 votes (confirmed above)  \n   * 2008: 37.10\u202f% \u2013 20,528 votes (runner\u2011up)  \n   * 2011: 30.73\u202f% \u2013 17,890 votes (runner\u2011up)  \n   * 2015: 43.33\u202f% \u2013 26,342 votes (runner\u2011up)\u3010commentary: 4\u2020source=2\u2020chunks=0,1,2,3,4,5,6\u3011  \n\nThe calculation of the vote\u2011share gaps uses the percentage figures above, yielding the 44.72\u202f% gap for 2004 as the maximum. Therefore, the runner\u2011up\u2019s vote count in that election\u2014that which corresponds to the largest 1st\u2011vs\u20113rd share difference\u2014is **19,524 votes**."